  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA broad upper trough is progged to persist across the\nMidwest/Great Lakes/Upper Ohio Valley region through Saturday.\nOne shortwave is progged to cross the region this morning with \nshowers and a few thunderstorms, especially north of PIT. \nExisting cloud cover associated with this shortwave could impact\nthe amount of diurnal destabilization this afternoon. ML CAPE \nis progged to range from 1000-1750 j/kg, depending on the amount\nsurface heating with breaks in the clouds. 0-6km shear is \nexpected to be slightly higher today than yesterday, around \n30kt. HREF indicates a DCAPE between 600 and 800 j/kg, with a \npocket of dry air aloft. Gusty wind will be possible in any of \nthe more organized storms. ML does indicate some potential for a\ndamaging wind threat as well. PWATS are also expected to be \nnear 1.8 inches. Heavy downpours will again be possible in some \nof the thunderstorms, with a potential for isolated flash \nflooding.\n\nThe intensity of the convection should wane this evening as\ndiurnal instability diminishes, though and additional vort max\nwithin the broad trough could maintain some showers/isolated\nthunderstorms overnight. These showers/storms should continue\ninto early Saturday as the shortwave completes its passage.\nAfter a lull in the activity for part of the day, a stronger \nshortwave within the trough is progged to cross the Upper Ohio \nValley region late Saturday into Saturday night, with additional\nshowers and thunderstorms, along with the continued potential \nfor gusty wind and heavy downpours. \n\nGuidance continues to trend toward a dry forecast on Sunday,\nwith brief shortwave ridging behind the stronger Saturday\nshortwave. Dry air and some capping should preclude convection\nthrough the day. \n\nThe broad trough is reestablished across the Great Lakes and\nUpper Ohio Valley region early next week, with daily shower and\nthunderstorm chances returning. Currently, ML indicates a\ngreater potential for severe weather during this period, though\nsignificant uncertainty remains in the details.",
